Part A: U-Boot Build and Deployment

1- Define what you Know about the bootloader? It's a low level trusted program executed immediately after the processor reset
	a) initializes HW (DRAM, clocks, uart)
	b) Configures memory controller
	c) Loads kernel into RAM
	d) pass boot parameters like DTB
	e) Jumps to Image
2- Draw and Explain the exact boot chain on Raspberry Pi from power-on until you see the U-Boot prompt.
boot chain: (it uses GPU-first boot flow) BootROM -> bootcode.bin -> start.elf -> U-boot -> Kernel
	a) BootROM: runs on the GPU, basic init HW (DRAM) then try to find filename "bootcode.bin" from bootable partition and load it into GPU memory
	b) bootcode.bin: it initalizes RPI HW (full DRAM init), sets up memory controller, then loads start.elf
	c) start.elf: main bootloader also runs on GPU, parses configuration from config.txt, allocates RAM between GPU and CPU loads U-boot if exist, otherwise kernel
	d) U-boot: initalizes ARM side HW, loads kernel, loads dtb, executes arm booting sequence
	e) kernel: mounts root filesystem then starts init process

PC: Power on -> bootROM -> bios -> GRUB -> kernel -> init process

3- What is the difference between U-Boot and GRUB ?
U-Boot: for embedded boards, basic file system, low level HW init, No GUI
GRUB: for x86 architecture, supports advanced file system, no HW init, offers GUI

4- What files must be placed in the Raspberry Pi boot partition to boot U-Boot, and define what is the important of each of them?
	a) bootcode.bin: it's extremely small and can't initialize full DRAM, without bootcode.bin the boot process stops immediately
	b) start.elf: this is the real firmware of the Rbpi, controls the entire early boot process before arm CPU starts exeuction
	c)config.txt: without it the firmware would try to boot hte Linux kernel directly instead of u-boot
	d) fixup.dat: used by start.elf, adjusts mem layout, applies mem fixups based on GPU memory split, ensures correct RAM layout between GPU and CPU
	e) u-boot.bin: this is the actual BL u built, it takes control after GPU firmware finishes
	f) .dtb file: linux kernel doesn't hardcode board HW, it relies on the DTB for HW discovery and peripheral init, without it the kernel can't initialize peripherals
	g) Image: it initializes drivers, mounts root filesystem, starts init process

